WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.149 --> 00:00:01.149
Hi there!

00:00:01.149 --> 00:00:06.120
I’ve been active in what you might call
the field of artificial intelligence for a

00:00:06.120 --> 00:00:07.120
while now.

00:00:07.120 --> 00:00:11.950
Of course, Ms. Coffee Bean knew everything
about the technology behind AI-based search

00:00:11.950 --> 00:00:15.730
engines,
but I honestly didn’t have any deeper thoughts

00:00:15.730 --> 00:00:20.779
about what it means to actually implement
a search engine, ever!

00:00:20.779 --> 00:00:26.759
But I was approached by SeMI Technologies
that made an open-source vector search engine

00:00:26.759 --> 00:00:27.759
called Weaviate.

00:00:27.759 --> 00:00:30.520
[scratched disk sound] A vector whaat?

00:00:30.520 --> 00:00:32.420
Yes, exactly.

00:00:32.420 --> 00:00:36.930
In this video we will explain the general
mechanism of modern search engines.

00:00:36.930 --> 00:00:42.540
It’s especially important to know if I am
ever going to make my private custom search

00:00:42.540 --> 00:00:46.990
engine for my super-secret Ms. Coffee Bean
data.

00:00:46.990 --> 00:00:52.510
Starting from the data, we will go through
and explain the most important steps:

00:00:52.510 --> 00:00:57.890
transforming the database into a vector database
with a machine learning model like FastText

00:00:57.890 --> 00:00:59.280
or Tarnsformers;

00:00:59.280 --> 00:01:00.870
then querying the database.

00:01:00.870 --> 00:01:05.290
Thanks to SeMI Technologies for sponsoring
this educational video!

00:01:05.290 --> 00:01:08.720
Ms. Coffee Bean are you ready to do the explainy
bits?

00:01:08.720 --> 00:01:11.080
Ok, let’s go!

00:01:11.080 --> 00:01:16.650
Search engines are the algorithms helping
you search over large sets of documents or

00:01:16.650 --> 00:01:19.760
other kinds of data – and do it fast.

00:01:19.760 --> 00:01:25.290
These documents can be either from the Internet
or the documents a company or organization

00:01:25.290 --> 00:01:27.890
has on their private servers.

00:01:27.890 --> 00:01:33.159
When search engines first appeared, they were
not the “clever” AI-powered beasts of

00:01:33.159 --> 00:01:35.560
today – we’ll get to those in a minute.

00:01:35.560 --> 00:01:41.390
The first search engines were relatively simple
implementations of full-text-based search.

00:01:41.390 --> 00:01:47.600
This is very similar to how we look something
up in a dictionary: If you searched for “computer”,

00:01:47.600 --> 00:01:52.780
the algorithm behind the search engine went
and matched strings to find occurrences of

00:01:52.780 --> 00:01:55.810
the word “computer” on the Internet (or
text database).

00:01:55.810 --> 00:02:01.920
There were clever tricks: For example, to
generalize from “computers” to “computer”,

00:02:01.920 --> 00:02:04.030
word inflections got removed.

00:02:04.030 --> 00:02:08.700
Or simple word distances were employed – that
you also see in spell-checkers

00:02:08.700 --> 00:02:14.599
– like the Levenshtein metric that measures
the minimum number of single-character edits

00:02:14.599 --> 00:02:17.170
needed to change one word into the other.

00:02:17.170 --> 00:02:22.230
But you see, there are many reasons to let
these search engines evolve into something

00:02:22.230 --> 00:02:23.230
smarter.

00:02:23.230 --> 00:02:28.901
What if, with “computer” I do not search
for a PC or Mac or a compute cluster, but

00:02:28.901 --> 00:02:34.769
what was originally meant by the word computer:
a human person – usually a woman – that

00:02:34.769 --> 00:02:39.540
used to do computations before digital computers
were invented.

00:02:39.540 --> 00:02:45.169
In this ambiguous case, how should the search
algorithm know which meaning of the word I

00:02:45.169 --> 00:02:46.169
intended?

00:02:46.169 --> 00:02:51.299
Taking the most frequent meaning would make
the search engine insensitive to rarer use

00:02:51.299 --> 00:02:53.189
cases of words.

00:02:53.189 --> 00:02:57.689
Even more, think of what happens if I search
something that is not even retrievable by

00:02:57.689 --> 00:03:02.629
simple string matching, like when I ask a
question: Who is the one who has the most

00:03:02.629 --> 00:03:03.629
money?

00:03:03.629 --> 00:03:09.319
How should the string-matching algorithm know
that “most money” means “richest”

00:03:09.319 --> 00:03:13.109
or that “the one” refers to “Coffee
Beans”?

00:03:13.109 --> 00:03:16.569
Or even worse, what if we try to do an image
search?

00:03:16.569 --> 00:03:20.670
These two images might look the same, but
if we look closer,

00:03:20.670 --> 00:03:27.029
bit by bit, they are different because the
same content is encoded differently.

00:03:27.029 --> 00:03:33.099
So, if we search for an image in a database
or on the internet, we might quickly fail

00:03:33.099 --> 00:03:34.790
if we just match strings.

00:03:34.790 --> 00:03:40.709
We need a search engine that is more than
just a dictionary lookup and is aware of content

00:03:40.709 --> 00:03:41.909
and context.

00:03:41.909 --> 00:03:46.709
So let’s bring in the heavy artillery of
today’s AI-powered search engines.

00:03:46.709 --> 00:03:51.959
Well, “AI” is a little of a buzzword here:
calling them for what they really are,

00:03:51.959 --> 00:03:57.389
modern search engines are vector search engines
over vector databases.

00:03:57.389 --> 00:04:02.949
Vector databases are a way of organizing the
documents in the database – in meaningful

00:04:02.949 --> 00:04:08.180
vectors! – to be able to search through
them with queries that are vectorized beforehand

00:04:08.180 --> 00:04:09.180
too.

00:04:09.180 --> 00:04:14.680
In contrast, things in a dictionary are organized
alphabetically, which makes sense when searching

00:04:14.680 --> 00:04:16.060
by string-matching.

00:04:16.060 --> 00:04:21.400
But vector databases allow us to organize
things even better: based on the context in

00:04:21.400 --> 00:04:23.200
which information finds itself.

00:04:23.200 --> 00:04:28.280
So, we have a new system for organizing our
database (or the indexed Internet), based

00:04:28.280 --> 00:04:30.560
on the principle of context:

00:04:30.560 --> 00:04:34.540
words that occur in the same context, belong
together.

00:04:34.540 --> 00:04:40.620
We have here two examples of how words that
are different, live in the same context.

00:04:40.620 --> 00:04:45.319
It becomes clear that the computer which I
turned off, is different from the computer

00:04:45.319 --> 00:04:48.860
as the job description that Ms. Coffee Bean’s
grandmother had.

00:04:48.860 --> 00:04:54.860
Of course, one example is often not representative,
so we look at more and more examples like

00:04:54.860 --> 00:04:57.700
these,
and we can set up a graph where “computer”

00:04:57.700 --> 00:05:02.669
is close to “PC” and “Mac”
and the jobs are close to “computer” too,

00:05:02.669 --> 00:05:06.430
but “PC” and the jobs should be farther
away from one another.

00:05:06.430 --> 00:05:12.840
If we take in all the other words used on
all webpages or in our own database, we can

00:05:12.840 --> 00:05:14.530
grow a big graph.

00:05:14.530 --> 00:05:21.009
A dictionary was in a sense one-dimensional,
as things follow one another only alphabetically:

00:05:21.009 --> 00:05:24.710
the first character was our only feature.

00:05:24.710 --> 00:05:30.460
But now we organize our database entries in
this graph where things have more than one

00:05:30.460 --> 00:05:35.669
feature: one feature might be describing how
much of a “job” something is;

00:05:35.669 --> 00:05:41.300
the other feature might describe how human
something is -- from "0 human to very human".

00:05:41.300 --> 00:05:46.509
With each feature or dimension that we add,
the more characteristics we can assign for

00:05:46.509 --> 00:05:47.509
a data point.

00:05:47.509 --> 00:05:51.990
Therefore, to what we have cramped here into
a two-dimensional visualization, we can actually

00:05:51.990 --> 00:05:53.639
give more space,

00:05:53.639 --> 00:05:59.159
where all the features, possibly hundreds,
each occupy a dimension in a vector space

00:05:59.159 --> 00:06:03.270
to really flesh out the complex contextual
relationships.

00:06:03.270 --> 00:06:09.129
Hereby, each word becomes a vector encoding
the exact position of the word in this high-dimensional

00:06:09.129 --> 00:06:10.129
space.

00:06:10.129 --> 00:06:15.439
To explain this on even higher levels: Imagine
you’re going to the supermarket:

00:06:15.439 --> 00:06:21.050
in the classical search engine case, it’s
like the supermarket was a long row of products

00:06:21.050 --> 00:06:24.830
all organized after the first letter in the
alphabet.

00:06:24.830 --> 00:06:30.259
But the problem there is, that if we go and
find the apples, next to the apples there

00:06:30.259 --> 00:06:35.610
are bongos
and not other fruit like pears or strawberries.

00:06:35.610 --> 00:06:41.379
Modern vector databases are just giving things
more space, for modelling relationships better.

00:06:41.379 --> 00:06:46.319
In each vector we can store the position of
each data point in this vector space encoding

00:06:46.319 --> 00:06:48.479
complex relationships in between.

00:06:48.479 --> 00:06:53.490
So after we encoded everything in a meaningful
vector, we now have a vector database.

00:06:53.490 --> 00:06:59.990
And of course, we do not have to do this encoding
of words into vectors by hand and we also

00:06:59.990 --> 00:07:03.759
do not have to come up with features or vector
entries ourselves,

00:07:03.759 --> 00:07:08.860
because there are readily available machine
learning models for this.

00:07:08.860 --> 00:07:13.039
Proprietary search engines, of course do not
let you choose the vectorizer.

00:07:13.039 --> 00:07:18.389
But customizable search engines like Weaviate
do give you the choice: you could take their

00:07:18.389 --> 00:07:25.060
custom one based on FastText called the Contextionary,
or the Transformers module, or a ResNet (for

00:07:25.060 --> 00:07:29.530
images), or you could add your own machine
learning models to vectorize with.

00:07:29.530 --> 00:07:34.280
But for this explanation, we will resort to
Ms. Coffee Bean’s favorite vectorization

00:07:34.280 --> 00:07:35.560
method:

00:07:35.560 --> 00:07:38.350
transformers that are state-of-the-art at
this.

00:07:38.350 --> 00:07:43.419
They are reading in lots of documents, like
your whole database, the whole Wikipedia or

00:07:43.419 --> 00:07:46.879
the whole Internet – not kidding.

00:07:46.879 --> 00:07:49.979
After they have read in all sentences in the
input data,

00:07:49.979 --> 00:07:56.060
they corrupt their input by deleting words
and then they try to reconstruct those words.

00:07:56.060 --> 00:08:02.070
The words are all represented by random vectors
at first, so initially the output looks random

00:08:02.070 --> 00:08:03.340
too.

00:08:03.340 --> 00:08:08.380
But example after example, these transformers
start to learn something about language by

00:08:08.380 --> 00:08:15.039
deleting, reconstructing words, and updating
their vectors to better – many many times,

00:08:15.039 --> 00:08:19.910
until they start to encode patterns in context-dependent
vectors.

00:08:19.910 --> 00:08:25.939
These patterns reflect which words occur together
in these many, many sentences, and which context

00:08:25.939 --> 00:08:27.410
words have.

00:08:27.410 --> 00:08:32.440
A kind of self-organization occurs where vectors
in this vector space start to cluster together

00:08:32.440 --> 00:08:37.240
based on how their respective words behaved
in the input data.

00:08:37.240 --> 00:08:43.040
Such a model has to be trained once and we
can now feed any kind of text into it, without

00:08:43.040 --> 00:08:47.650
the need of retraining and can encode our
whole database.

00:08:47.650 --> 00:08:53.580
Now, with all the words indexed in our database
in vector form, we can take in our query,

00:08:53.580 --> 00:08:58.020
pass it through the same model to produce
us vectors for the query too.

00:08:58.020 --> 00:09:00.070
And now we can compare the vectors.

00:09:00.070 --> 00:09:05.310
If we search for “female computer” the
vector for “computer” in the query is

00:09:05.310 --> 00:09:10.350
informed by its context “female”,
therefore, we will retrieve the database entries

00:09:10.350 --> 00:09:14.870
that also figure the word computer in the
same job context.

00:09:14.870 --> 00:09:18.130
Through the same mechanism, we can answer
questions too.

00:09:18.130 --> 00:09:25.030
The cool thing is that what we have explained
here with text is possible with images, videos,

00:09:25.030 --> 00:09:30.970
speech or other kinds of data too, especially
because these vectorizing models, the transformers,

00:09:30.970 --> 00:09:36.380
can handle all these kinds of data to compute
content and context-aware vectors.

00:09:36.380 --> 00:09:39.840
For more about this, watch some of our videos
on multimodality.

00:09:39.840 --> 00:09:45.080
Well, this is what happens on a high level
in AI-powered search engines.

00:09:45.080 --> 00:09:51.580
Yeah, it’s cool especially because it does
not need human supervision that would be impossible

00:09:51.580 --> 00:09:58.200
at these large scales, but admittedly it’s
less magical when one really knows what happens,

00:09:58.200 --> 00:09:59.290
right?

00:09:59.290 --> 00:10:05.380
We are not really telling you that the Google
or Bing or any other search engines work exactly

00:10:05.380 --> 00:10:06.380
this way:

00:10:06.380 --> 00:10:11.380
The algorithms behind them are proprietary
and not disclosed.

00:10:11.380 --> 00:10:16.270
But judging by their capabilities and especially
their failure modes, there is a definitely

00:10:16.270 --> 00:10:20.330
a vector database and a vector search engine
behind them.

00:10:20.330 --> 00:10:26.150
The real lessons about these modern, vector
search engines, we can know from OPEN-source

00:10:26.150 --> 00:10:27.150
projects.

00:10:27.150 --> 00:10:29.230
Weaviate is such an open-source vector search
engine.

00:10:29.230 --> 00:10:33.770
You know, OPEN, where can one inspect the
code, open the documentation.

00:10:33.770 --> 00:10:39.280
This is important to have not only because
you know what is going on in there, but also

00:10:39.280 --> 00:10:43.830
in the situation when you are a company or
an institution with millions of documents

00:10:43.830 --> 00:10:45.630
and want to search through them.

00:10:45.630 --> 00:10:50.580
For example, what if you are a hospital with
millions of clinical data samples?

00:10:50.580 --> 00:10:52.840
Or a factory with product data?

00:10:52.840 --> 00:10:55.470
Or a shop with lots of customer data?

00:10:55.470 --> 00:10:59.570
Post all your data on the Internet to be searchable
by Google Search?

00:10:59.570 --> 00:11:01.670
Of course, that’s a no-go.

00:11:01.670 --> 00:11:07.920
It's estimated that more than 99% of data
is not publicly available, so if you possess

00:11:07.920 --> 00:11:12.670
a part of that data, you would either have
to implement a search engine yourself

00:11:12.670 --> 00:11:17.740
or take and contribute to the open-source
work that was already done.

00:11:17.740 --> 00:11:22.520
Having control about the search engine on
your data is important also concerning the

00:11:22.520 --> 00:11:28.300
vectorizing model we talked about just before,
that takes in data and transforms it into

00:11:28.300 --> 00:11:29.700
vectors.

00:11:29.700 --> 00:11:33.550
Because that model is good with types of data
it has already seen.

00:11:33.550 --> 00:11:39.630
If it was trained on webpages, it is good
at judging internet forum text, normal websites,

00:11:39.630 --> 00:11:44.480
but what if you have technical engineering
terms or medical data in your database?

00:11:44.480 --> 00:11:50.021
In this case, you need to take this model
and fine-tune it on that medical data, meaning

00:11:50.021 --> 00:11:53.580
that you train it further by showing it medical
text.

00:11:53.580 --> 00:11:58.950
It takes it in and self-trains by following
the same procedure as before: it deletes words

00:11:58.950 --> 00:12:03.360
and tries to reconstruct the text based on
the corrupted version only.

00:12:03.360 --> 00:12:08.930
This then lets it extract patterns on this
new domain of data, like medical texts, or

00:12:08.930 --> 00:12:10.140
other domains.

00:12:10.140 --> 00:12:14.430
Weaviate even has a simpler solution where
for every undefined word, you can provide

00:12:14.430 --> 00:12:20.230
the definition of that word and use this as
context, and you do not need to further train

00:12:20.230 --> 00:12:21.230
anything.

00:12:21.230 --> 00:12:24.220
So to recapitulate what we need for a vector
search engine:

00:12:24.220 --> 00:12:29.501
First, we need the data, then we need the
machine learning model (like the transformer

00:12:29.501 --> 00:12:33.971
but doesn’t need to)
which we use to vectorize the data, so our

00:12:33.971 --> 00:12:37.670
database is now a vector database.

00:12:37.670 --> 00:12:39.510
And we are ready to search through it!

00:12:39.510 --> 00:12:44.331
All we need is a query that we should also
vectorize with the same machine learning model

00:12:44.331 --> 00:12:49.530
because we now want to ask which vector in
the database is closest to our query.

00:12:49.530 --> 00:12:54.180
But Ms. Coffee Bean is not the best coder
when it comes to handling tons of data without

00:12:54.180 --> 00:12:58.910
running out of memory and waiting for ages
for the search to complete.

00:12:58.910 --> 00:13:03.600
Searching through a lot of data fast is not
an easy thing, admittedly, so here is where

00:13:03.600 --> 00:13:08.680
she wants to rely on Weaviate
that has already figured it out how to scale

00:13:08.680 --> 00:13:12.410
the vector search for millions of vectors
in milliseconds.

00:13:12.410 --> 00:13:16.470
Okay, Ms. Coffee Bean, you are allowed to
check it out!

00:13:16.470 --> 00:13:19.930
I mean, why not, it’s open source after
all!

00:13:19.930 --> 00:13:24.680
So now, Ms. Coffee Bean, are you ready to
find out the truth?

00:13:24.680 --> 00:13:30.170
We need to ask: “Where can I get the best
coffee for my AI coffee break?”

00:13:30.170 --> 00:13:32.060
Aa, around the globe.

00:13:32.060 --> 00:13:34.320
Exactly, that’s the spirit!

00:13:34.320 --> 00:13:38.810
We hope you liked our explanation of modern
search engines in general.

00:13:38.810 --> 00:13:43.530
If you are interested in a more technical
presentation about Weaviate in particular,

00:13:43.530 --> 00:13:48.750
check out the video in the upper right corner
– also linked in the description below.

00:13:48.750 --> 00:13:52.070
[whisper] Do not forget to like and subscribe!

00:13:52.070 --> 00:13:53.070
See you!

00:13:53.070 --> 00:13:53.740
Okay, bye!

